{
  "dom_id": "step-q94847",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $3{t}^{2}-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}-t=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot t+3\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
